WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.367
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.145 --> 00:00:10.020
SPEAKER: At Google, our
services and products

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.510
run on a globally-distributed
computing system

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.940
made up of many machines
and data centers.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.640
They're all linked to a
large-scale network that's

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.310
core to what makes Google
services, including Google

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.760
Cloud, run.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.110
So how does Google's data
center networking work?

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.230
First, you need to understand
the size of our network.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.540
Google's network
supports a 1 petabyte

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.610
per second
bisectional bandwidth.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.970
To put that in perspective,
you could transfer the entirety

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.570
of Wikipedia in under a
second, or enough bandwidth

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.670
to read the entire
scanned contents

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.840
of the Library of Congress in
less than 1/10 of a second.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.310
While I could talk
about the fiber

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.920
optic cables or physical
switches we use,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.270
the real power comes from how
we manage traffic at scale.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.430
See, the networking
between data centers

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.620
is built with diverse
pathways of fiber

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.350
interconnecting data
centers, points of presence,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.709
or local network access
points, and even continents

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:06.480
through subsea cables.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.160
We need to ensure
data centers can

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.020
talk to each other
for resilience

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.620
so we can serve traffic close
to our users and customers.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.370
To do that, we use our
global wide area network.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.460
Our goal is to create enough
redundancy and capacity

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.150
of the overall network to reduce
traffic congestion in any one

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:28.030
area and withstand
any network failures.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.790
That's why at our
data centers, we've

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.370
invested in not just one
fiber link out of a building,

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:36.420
but many fiber links to provide
more paths and redundancy

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.790
to our long-haul network
or wide area network.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:41.910
So what's happening
within the data centers

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.520
once traffic passes
through these links?

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.620
Our data center network
fabric is made up

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.500
of a closed hierarchical
switching fabric we designed

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.320
called Jupiter, which
connects hundreds of thousands

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.900
of machines across
data center buildings.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.840
Machines at the data center
have physical connections,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.840
or switching, into
Jupiter, and all traffic

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.580
flowing through
Jupiter is controlled

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.550
by a central
software-defined controller.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:09.120
Jupiter's able to provide such
large bandwidth because it's

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.820
nonblocking, which
means it can handle

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.460
routing a request to
any free output port

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.800
without interfering
other traffic.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.730
This means it can scale or
burst with extremely low latency

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.070
and is capable of
building resilience.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.780
If something in
the fabric breaks,

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.650
it can handle disruptions.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.750
So where does Google Cloud
traffic fit into all of this?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.270
Andromeda is a virtualized
software-defined network

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.520
built on top of
Jupiter, giving you

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.250
your very own slice of our
massive global switching

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.150
fabric.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.600
Andromeda enables you to
deploy a virtual private cloud

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.330
network, giving you both
functional and performance

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.330
isolation and a high
degree of security.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.550
With its global control
plane, high speed

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.400
on-host virtual switch,
and packet processors,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.500
you can burst thousands
of stateful machines

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.080
online in minutes or
deploy firewall rules

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.480
across thousands of
machines immediately

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.490
without chokepoints.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.280
And because Compute and
Storage are separated and talk

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.870
to each other
through the network,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.730
they can scale
independently so you

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.610
get virtually unlimited
storage and stateless

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.470
resilient compute.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.420
Whether you're processing
petabytes of data

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.690
in seconds using BigQuery,
running consistent databases

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:22.380
across regions using Spanner,
or autoscaling GKE clusters

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.710
across zones, Google's data
centers and global network

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.340
backbone together make these
technologies impressive.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.930
If you want to learn
more, check out

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.980
cloud.google.com/networking.

